  <title>Profiling zsh: a Python plugin was adding 190 ms to every command</title>
  <link>https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-10-02-zsh-startup-and-command-lag/</link>
  <pubDate>Fri, 02 Oct 2026 23:15:00 &#43;0900</pubDate>
  <author>william</author>
  <guid>https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-10-02-zsh-startup-and-command-lag/</guid>
  <description><![CDATA[<div class="featured-image"><img loading="eager" src="https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-10-02-zsh-startup-and-command-lag/featured-image.png" alt="Profiling zsh: a Python plugin was adding 190 ms to every command" title="Profiling zsh: a Python plugin was adding 190 ms to every command" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"/></div><p>After <a href="/2026-10-02-tmux-lag-round-2/">fixing tmux</a>, I looked at
the shell running inside it. My zsh config had grown over a few years to about
30 plugins and Oh My Zsh snippets on top of zinit and Powerlevel10k. It felt a
little heavy, but nothing stood out.</p>
<p>Measured properly, it was slow in three separate ways, each with its own cause:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th style="text-align:right">before</th>
<th style="text-align:right">after</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>startup to a usable shell</td>
<td style="text-align:right">267 ms</td>
<td style="text-align:right">101 ms</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>added to <strong>every command</strong></td>
<td style="text-align:right">188 ms</td>
<td style="text-align:right">3 ms</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>added to <strong>every prompt</strong></td>
<td style="text-align:right">15 ms</td>
<td style="text-align:right">4 ms</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>This post covers how I measured each one, the fixes, and how I checked that the
fixes didn&rsquo;t change anything else. That check caught two mistakes of my own.</p>]]></description>
</item>
